CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a three-dimensional plot of a single space curve rendered in dark blue against labeled coordinate axes. The vertical z-axis is graduated with ticks and numerals at 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12. The x-axis runs toward the lower left with ticks labeled 2 and 4 in front and -2 and -4 behind, and the y-axis runs toward the lower right with the same labeling, 2 and 4 on the near side and -2 and -4 on the far side. The curve spirals counterclockwise around the vertical axis while climbing steadily, completing roughly two full turns between the xy-plane and the top of the frame above z = 12; its circular cross-section has radius 4, so each loop sweeps out past the ticks marked 4 on both horizontal axes. Two arrowheads placed on the ascending curve show the direction of travel. Across the upper right, in bold, the formula r(t) = 4cost i + 4sint j + t k is printed. Holding the loop radius constant while the height grows in step with the parameter is precisely what produces the spring-like shape. The plot displays the graph of a vector-valued function whose circular motion in the horizontal plane combines with uniform vertical rise to trace a helix.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - vertical z-axis drawn as an upward arrow through the origin, with tick marks and numerals 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12
  - x-axis drawn as a line receding to the lower-left with an arrowhead, ticks labeled 4 and 2 on the near (lower-left) side and -2, -4 on the far (upper-right) side, bold italic label 'x' at the lower-left arrow tip
  - y-axis drawn as a line receding to the lower-right with an arrowhead, ticks labeled 2 and 4 on the near (lower-right) side and -2, -4 on the far (upper-left) side, bold italic label 'y' at the lower-right arrow tip
  - dark blue helix of radius 4 spiraling counterclockwise (viewed from +z) and rising from z=0 to just above z=12, about two full turns
  - two arrowheads on the ascending curve indicating direction of travel: one on the lower loop near y>0 around z~1, one on the upper loop near y>0 around z~6
  - bold equation label 'r(t) = 4cos t i + 4sin t j + t k' placed in the upper-right of the frame
  - moving point (marker) on the curve at the current parameter t
  - faint radial guide segment from the z-axis to the moving point and a vertical dashed drop line from the point to the xy-plane (shows the constant radius 4 and the height z = t)
  - live readout of t, x = 4cos t, y = 4sin t, z = t

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World units equal graph units. Helix: x=4cos t, y=4sin t, z=t for t in [0, 12.8] (about 2.04 turns), radius exactly 4, top just above z=12. Axes: z-axis from z=-0.5 to z=13.5 (arrow at top, ticks/numerals at 2,4,6,8,10,12 on the left side of the axis); x-axis spans -5 to 5 along the screen-lower-left/upper-right diagonal with the POSITIVE end toward the lower left (label 'x' there, ticks 2 and 4 on that near side, -2 and -4 on the far upper-right side); y-axis spans -5 to 5 along the screen-lower-right/upper-left diagonal with the POSITIVE end toward the lower right (label 'y' there, ticks 2 and 4 near, -2 and -4 far). Tick marks are short strokes drawn perpendicular to each horizontal axis in the xy-plane. Origin at world (0,0,0), placed roughly at the lower-third center of the frame so the helix occupies the upper two-thirds. Curve color dark navy-blue #1F4E79 / #16537e, line width ~2.5 px; axes and tick labels black, thin (~1 px); equation text black bold, upper right. Direction arrowheads tangent to the curve at t≈1.2 (z≈1.2, near the front-right of the lowest loop) and t≈6.0 (z≈6.0, front-right of the second loop), both pointing in the direction of increasing t (counterclockwise seen from +z, i.e. up-and-to-the-right on screen). Moving point: filled sphere/disc radius ~0.25, orange-red #c0392b for contrast. Radial guide segment from (0,0,z) to the point, and dashed vertical line from the point down to (x,y,0), both light gray ~40% opacity.

